-  prints the commit that wrote the report; its proof
-  sources are the ones the report was built from, and
-  python3.12 scripts/assumption_receipt_fingerprint.py
-  run at the tag prints the corpus digest above
-Manuscript source for this edition:
-  monograph/monograph.tex at the release commit; a file
-  can't print its own checksum, so take it with
-  git rev-parse v4.0.0:monograph/monograph.tex
-\end{verbatim}
-
-A commit can't print its own hash either, and that's why the block names the release by its tag and
-gives the command that turns the tag into a hash. The corpus digest ties the report to the
-sources without trusting the tag. It is a SHA-256 hash, computed by
-\texttt{scripts/assumption\_receipt\_fingerprint.py}, over this input, in this order: a fixed
-label; the first line Coq's compiler prints for its version; then, for every proof file the
-project lists in \texttt{coq/\_CoqProject} and every proof file of the vendored library, sorted
-by path, the file's path and its text with comments removed, each run of white space shortened to
-one space, and the wording of the strings in its \texttt{claim\_not\_imply} audit notes erased; then
-the project file \texttt{coq/\_CoqProject} as written, and the five scripts that scope, generate, run,
-aggregate and fingerprint the audit, each read as a stream of Python tokens with its comments dropped.
-The version-two fingerprint records token names instead of Python's internal numeric token IDs;
-the same corpus gives the same digest under Python 3.12 and 3.13. Each item is followed by a zero
-byte. So an edit to a Coq comment or to its layout leaves the digest alone, and any other edit to
-a proof file moves it, apart from the audit-note wording described above.
-
-That report belongs to the identified revision. A change to the proof sources beyond their comments and
-layout changes the corpus digest; regenerate the report before comparing a changed source tree.
-With those installed, run:
-
-\begin{verbatim}
-python3 scripts/reproduce_coq.py
-\end{verbatim}
-
-That copies the sources into a fresh directory and works only from the copy: it builds the
-part of the vendored Coq Library of Undecidability Proofs the proofs use, regenerates the build
-file, rebuilds every proof from source, and runs Coq's independent checker over every module the
-project lists. Extra probe files that re-check named theorems can be added with \texttt{--probe};
-none run by default. It reuses no compiled proof from the
-checkout, and it writes its pass file only after every stage passes. A failed run resumes
-from the snapshot it captured, and a change to the sources needs a fresh run:
-
-\begin{verbatim}
-python3 scripts/reproduce_coq.py --resume \
-  --output artifacts/reproduction/RUN_DIRECTORY
-\end{verbatim}
-
-To inspect a result, read its full statement, its derivation and the definitions it uses.
-The reproduction run checks the formal source; comparing that source with the argument on
-the page remains a separate task.
-
-Each other result this book reports has its own command, run from the root of the same
-checkout. The tests that need Coq skip without coqc 8.18 and a compiled tree; the two
-environment variables that point them at one are named in tests/realize\_harness.py.
